In this experiment, I learned how to estimate the cost and usage requirements of an AI application before using it in a real project. When we develop an application using an AI model, we need to understand how many requests users may send, how many tokens will be used, and how much money may be required. For this experiment, I created a Python program using a class named FeatureBudget. First, I prepared the .env file to store the required values, such as daily requests, average input tokens, average output tokens, API pricing, RPM limit, TPM limit, and monthly budget. Then I created the budget.py file and used the FeatureBudget class to read these values and perform the calculations. The program calculates the expected input and output tokens used per day and estimates the daily and monthly cost based on the configured token prices. It also checks whether the expected requests and token usage are within the given RPM and TPM limits. A safety margin is included to provide some extra capacity for unexpected usage. After completing the calculations, the program displays the estimated usage and cost along with PASS or REVIEW results for the different limits. I also used the test_budget.py file to check whether the calculations were working correctly and whether the program could identify situations where the budget or capacity limits were exceeded. I executed the program after installing the required packages and setting the configuration values in the .env file. Through this experiment, I understood why cost estimation and capacity planning are important before developing and deploying an AI-based application.
